## INTRODUCTION
Post-synthetic metal exchange has emerged as a powerful tool to construct metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) that are not accessible through direct synthesis. Moreover, MOFs synthesized through post-synthetic metal exchange have been shown to exhibit exotic coordination geometries,3 extraordinary stability,4 highly-active and selective catalytic reactivities,5 and tunable adsorptive properties.6 For example, the stability of the MOF PCN-426-Mg can be dramatically improved by metal exchange and oxidation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{2+}$ .4a The synthesis of Ni-MFU-4l via $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ exchange for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in MFU-4l represents another pivotal advancement,5a,7 as this material acts as the most active and selective heterogeneous catalyst to produce 1-butene by ethylene dimerization.
Although metal exchange in MOFs has been extensively studied, slow reaction kinetics constitute a frequently observed limitation.9 Some metal exchange reactions require several weeks to a year to reach thermodynamic equilibrium, and in many cases only a fraction of the native metal ions are replaced.2b,3,6a,b,10 For instance, in $Z\mathrm{n}$ -HKUST-1, a maximum of $56\%$ of the framework $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions could be replaced by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ after three months.10h In addition, exchange of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions in MOF-5 to reach the thermodynamic limit of $75\%$ exchange was shown to require one month.10c These examples emphasize the need for general and facile methods to control metal exchange rates, which necessitate a microscopic and mechanistic understanding of metal exchange reactions.
In principle, a metal exchange reaction should proceed through a series of three successive steps: (1) metal transport of an exogenous solvated metal ion into the framework crystal, (2) transmetallation of the exogenous metal ion for the endogenous metal ion and the corresponding back-exchange,10k and (3) departure of the endogenous metal ion from the crystal. Although transmetallation processes of certain metal exchange reactions have been thoroughly discussed,9,10c studies that focus on the metal transport process are scarce. Indeed, a detailed examination of metal transport, including information regarding kinetics and the diffusion pathway, would provide an important step toward a complete understanding of metal exchange reactions. Furthermore, synthetic control over the metal transport pathways could enable the deliberate construction of complex crystal architectures consisting of multiple materials with well-defined or well-blended heterojunctions and interfaces.
Herein, we report a 2D manganese-benzoquinoid framework and its metal exchange reactions with $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathsf{2}+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ . A comprehensive study of the metal transport process suggests the solvated exogenous metal ion can only diffuse through the 1D channel along the crystallographic axis, and such process is identified as the rate determining step. Moreover, this framework exhibits reversible dynamic structural behavior upon desolvation and subsequent resolvation. A total crystal volume change of $20\%$ is associated with this dynamic behavior, which is utilized to accelerate metal transport by up to 2000-fold, increase the exchange completeness from $57\%$ to $89\%$ , and generate metastable products in pure phases. In addition, by conducting partial metal exchange using fullysolvated crystals, a gradient vertical heterostructure, wherein two crystalline phases coexist within one crystal and are distributed in a gradient along the crystallographic $c$ axis, is obtained.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis and Structure of Manganese-Benzoquinoid Framework. Reaction of equimolar amounts of $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and chloranilic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{L})$ with five equivalents of $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)C l}$ in DMF at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $19\mathrm{~h~}$ produced brown hexagonal block-shaped crystals of $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)_{2}[M n_{2}L_{3}]}$ (1) suitable for single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction analysis. The phase purity of 1 was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, see Figure S1), optical microscopy (see Figure S2), and combustion elemental analysis (see Experimental Section in the Electronic Supporting Information (ESI)). While the synthesis of 1 was also recently reported employing a slow-oxidative layering technique, in that case 1 was isolated in the presence of an $\mathrm{[LMn(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ impurity and was not structurallycharacterized.
Compound 1 crystallized in the trigonal space group $P3\boldsymbol{\mathrm{l}}m$ . Within the structure, each $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ center resides in an octahedral coordination environment comprised of two O cis-disposed donors from each of three $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ linkers. This coordination mode gives rise to dianionic, honeycomb-like layers represented by a (6, 3)-connected net, with a closest interlayer $\mathbf{Mn}^{\cdots}\mathbf{Mn}$ distance of 10.248(2) $\textrm{\AA}$ (see Figure 1). These 2D layers, which feature a formula unit of $\left[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ , are stacked along the crystallographic $\scriptstyle{c}$ axis in an eclipsed conformation, with each $\mathrm{Et_{4}N^{\bar{+}}}$ cation situated equidistant from two Mn centers of adjacent layers (see Figure 1b). This stacking creates hexagonal one-dimensional channels along the $c$ axis, with a largest cross-channel $\mathbf{Mn}{\cdots}\mathbf{Mn}$ distance of 16.203(1) Å. The maximum pore diameter,13 defined as the maximum spherical probe that can fit in the framework, where Van der Waals radii are used for the framework atoms, was calculated to be $8.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , consistent with the pore size distribution based on the crystal structure (see Figure S3).14 1 is isostructural to the previously reported materials $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)_{2}[Z n_{2}L_{3}]}$ and $\underset{1}{\big(}\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathrm{N})_{2}[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}]$ , and to the analogous fluoranilate frameworks.11,15
The structure of 1 features mean $C^{\mathrm{-}}C$ and $C{\mathrm{-}}0$ distances of 1.44(1) and 1.254(7) Å, respectively. These values are similar to those observed in the materials containing analogous $\ [\boldsymbol{\mathrm{M}}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{L}}^{2-})_{3}]^{2-}$ layers (see Table 1),11,16 and therefore confirm the absence of ligand-based redox chemistry during the formation of 1. Moreover, the mean $\bf{M n}{-}\bf{O}$ distance of 2.158(3) $\textrm{\AA}$ confirms the presence of high-spin $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers. This observation contrasts with the spontaneous metal-to-ligand electrontransfer found in analogous Fe-based frameworks,15,16b,17 and likely reflects a more oxidative $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II/III}}}$ couple compared to $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II/II}}$
The geometric surface area of 1 was calculated to be 1153(5) $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ using a rolling nitrogen probe method,18 similar to that of 1175(29) $\mathbf{\bar{m}}^{2}/\mathbf{g}$ reported Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $(\mathrm{\bar{M}e_{2}\bar{N}H_{2}})_{2}[\mathrm{Fe_{2}L_{3}}]$ .19 Using a He insertion simulation method,20 the void fraction of 1 was calculated to be $45.66(7)\%$ . Experimentally, 1 adsorbed only a negligible amount of $\Nu_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ compared to simulated isotherms (see Figures S4 and S5). The discrepancy in the simulated and experimental gas adsorption isotherms suggests that the structure of 1, upon activation, may be different than what is depicted in the crystal structure, as is described below. For instance, a structural deformation may be associated with desolvation upon activation, giving rise to a closed structure as described below.
Metal Exchange. Soaking solvated crystals of 1 in a solution of $\mathrm{Co(ClO_{4})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ in DMF for 14 days at ambient temperature gave the metal-exchanged product $\mathrm{[Co(DMF)_{6}][C o_{2}L_{3}]}$ (2a) via a single-crystal-to-single-crystal (SC-SC) process. The mole fraction of Co in 2a was determined to be $99.614(3)\%$ by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES), demonstrating near quantitative exchange. The structure of 2a belongs to the hexagonal space group $P\mathfrak{I}\mathrm{{l}}m$ . While the 2D network in 2a is isostructural to that in 1, metal substitution results in a shortening of the M– O bonds. Specifically, the mean $\mathrm{Co-O}$ distance in 2a of 2.074(7) Å is $3.9\%$ shorter than that of 2.158(3) observed for Mn–O in 1 (see Table 1), and this value is indicative of highspin ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ .21 All other bond distances within the 2D network of 2a are identical to those of 1, indicating the absence of redox chemistry upon metal exchange. Since $\bar{\mathrm{L}}^{2-}$ is a relatively short bridging ligand compared to most reported MOFs, this change in the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}$ distance results in a considerable contraction of $2.7\%$ in each of the crystallographically equivalent $a$ and $b$ axes. In addition, upon metal exchange, the original $\mathrm{Et_{4}N}^{+}$ counterion was replaced by half of an equivalent of $\left[\mathrm{Co(DMF)_{6}}\right]^{2+}$ , leading to a contraction of the $c$ axis by $4.9\%$ , from 10.248(2) Å for 1 to 9.745(1) for 2a. This contraction is largely due to the placement of the $\mathrm{\Delta[Co(DMF)_{6}]^{2+}}$ within the 1D channel, rather than between two Co atoms of adjacent layers as observed for $\mathrm{Et_{4}N}^{+}$ in 1.
The observation of such large changes in the unit cell lengths upon metal exchange is unusual,23 and provides an opportunity to monitor the exchange process using ex situ PXRD analysis. As shown in Figure 2a, the intensities of the peaks belonging to 1 (orange ticks) gradually diminish over the course of the exchange reaction, while the peaks associated with 2a (purple ticks) concomitantly emerge.
Under these metal exchange conditions, a secondary crystalline phase appeared upon prolonged reaction times, with characteristic peaks at $10.87^{\circ}$ , $12.50^{\circ}$ , and $21.37^{\circ}$ . This unidentified phase presumably represents the thermodynamic product of the reaction between ${\dot{\mathrm{Co}}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ , as attempts to synthesize 2a or $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}[\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}]$ directly under solventothermal or at ambient conditions, including conditions where $\left[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ or $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ crystal seeds were added,24 invariably yielded this unknown product. In contrast, carrying out post-synthetic metal exchange enables access to the metastable phase 2a. This result emphasizes the utility of metal exchange for synthesizing materials that are otherwise not accessible through direct synthesis. Moreover, this observation eliminates the possibility that 2a forms via a dissolution–recrystallization mechanism, as such a mechanism is expected to exclusively produce the secondary phase.
In addition to X-ray diffraction, the metal exchange process can also be monitored by ICP-OES. As shown in Figure 2c, the mole fraction of Co gradually increased over the course of the reaction, reaching $99\%$ after 12 days. The time constant $t_{1/2}$ defined here as the reaction time required to reach $50\%$ exchange, was determined to be $98\mathrm{~h~}$ .
Similarly, soaking crystals of 1 in a solution of $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF for 12 days at ambient temperature generated the analogous material $\mathrm{[Zn(DMF)_{6}][Z n_{0.68}M n_{1.32}L_{3}]}$ (3a, see Figure 1b), with its $Z\mathrm{n}$ mole fraction determined to be $56.03(2)\%$ by ICP-OES. By monitoring the reaction using ex situ ICP-OES, a $t_{1/2}=79$ h was determined (see Figure 2d). Prolonged reaction time did not increase the Zn mole fraction, but instead led to the gradual dissolution of the solid. In addition, monitoring the $Z\mathrm{n}$ exchange by ex situ PXRD (see Figure 2b) revealed that both $\mathrm{[Mn_{2}L_{3}]}^{\bar{2}-}$ and $\left[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ phases are present throughout the reaction. Here, the diffraction peaks belonging to the $\left[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ phase (blue ticks) gradually increased in intensity over the course of the reaction. The incomplete exchange observed for $Z\mathrm{n}$ may stem in part from the similar and small electronegativity values for $Z\bar{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , leading to high lability of both metal ions.
Analogous to the metal exchange with Co to generate 2a, exchange with $Z\mathrm{n}$ also follows a SC-SC process. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction pattern of a single crystal of 3a exhibits two sets of diffraction spots that belong to the $\left[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ and $\left[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ phases, respectively (see Figures S6 and S7). According to these diffraction images, the two lattices exhibit the same orientation, with one set of diffraction spots systematically appearing at a slightly higher angle, and hence smaller lattice parameters $a$ and $b$ , relative to the other domain. These findings suggest that both $\left[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ and $\left[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]^{2-}$ lattices are present in a single domain in the crystal and grow in an epitaxial fashion. Finally, based on the $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction experiment discussed above, the possibility that 3a is a mixture of
